<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plagues of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as the Ten Plagues</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-exodus">Book of Exodus</a>, the Plagues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> are ten disasters that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a> inflicts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> to persuade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> to free the enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>. They run from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> turned to blood, frogs, gnats and flies through the death of livestock, boils, hail, locusts and three days of darkness, to the death of the firstborn. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> recite the plagues during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> Seder. Modern scholars broadly hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> is not a historical account of the origins of the Israelites. Biblical scholars have dismissed natural explanations, such as a volcanic eruption for the darkness, because the plagues' pattern, timing and control by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> mark them as supernatural in the text.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plagues-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Plagues of Egypt" width="330" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scenes_from_Exodus_f_16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
